ave this tremendous  impetus to to the union but now let's  look at the 60s  the 60s led to a lot of changes not  because of labor disruption not because  of economic failure but first of all  because of the fact that there had been  created  a small black middle class in the south  that  wanted to insist that the country live  up to its its liberal  values and how did they do it  they did it at the key point by  disrupting people's routines they sat  down in lunch counters and said we will  not move we shall not be moved and that  sit-in movement once again spread to  buses to to all kinds of  facilities where people sat in and they  disrupted the lives  of these people they could not in  birmingham for instance they boycott the  downtown they marched through the  downtown now no longer can you just go  downtown and shop can you go to your  office you got to worry something's  going to happen  the anti-war movement did the same thing  the anti-war movement disrupted the  routines of  administrators of faculty indeed of of  the likes of secretary of defense  mcnamara he could not go to harvard and  collect his honorary degree because  there were people out in the streets  protesting  jostling his car hissing and booing and  so on that is what  makes things happen now i'm not  advocating by this say for instance  violence i'm describing and i happen to  believe personally that the best kinds  of disruptions are non-violent direct  action but i'm saying but stepping back  from you know my advocacy there again  into the role of describer or analyst  as what i'm saying is that when we do  see change  what we see is in some way people's  routines have been disrupted and what  will cause that is economic dislocation  wars  sometimes will be some kind of an  accident that will disrupt people's  routines a major oil spill a major  radiation  clear accident like we're seeing now  that's how the union may force people  right it forces people out of their  routines and and also in the face of  these accidents what happens that's very  interesting in terms of our normal  everyday beliefs is that an accident  makes it very hard to manage the news  tremendous amount of what comes across  the media  is when  a spokesperson for the state department  or the president or whatever stands up  at this carefully staged press  conference and tells us what's happening  or is going to happen the corporation  releases a a statement saying we're now  going to build this kind of car whatever  in the face of an accident  they can't sort of get their  viewpoint they can't get their line  together and often will unravel for them  and we get a very revealing picture  then of of a power structure in the face  of a break in routine and of course  watergate was a nice example of that and  finally we end up of all things with the  tapes  of richard nixon's conversations in the  white house now that was over an  accident when a man that wasn't supposed  to be arrested was arrested  you lie about it you don't have your  story real straight and that leads to  another thing and it just kept  unraveling  in this wonderful kind of in that case  it was a disruption in their their  levels and it spread in a way that then  gave us this incredible uh information  about how the power structure operated  another uh example the way in which  we've uh disruption is important but  also we learned  much about the power structure involves  daniel ellsberg who was a policy analyst  for the defense department and  had worked for a think tank called the  rand corporation  had often been an advisor to major  policy makers at these discussion groups  ellsberg was from a union family  but had  gone on to go to harvard and become this  big expert  but in the face of the anti-war movement  it rekindled in him he he reported his  feelings of of  his background and he decided that the  war was so wrong that he was going to  reveal  the origins of this war  by  putting the pentagon papers out in the  general public and so in a word  we learned this amazing detail of of the  fact that of how of how the whole war  policy was made of the fact that they  were saying one thing and doing another  in other words they knew the problems  they were going to have winning that war  at the very time they were saying it was  going to be a lead pipe cinch  so but ellsberg just wasn't somebody  that suddenly decided to give these  papers out it was only the disruption in  the society that gradually got to him  in terms of kind of stripping away the  veneer that he had learned that made him  then re-identify  with people of  uh that were struggling in this society  so accident scandals  break routines and major social  disruptions  like a civil rights movement or a ghetto  uprising which involves the massive  movement of black people from  from  south to north that's one of the largest  migrations of the people in history in  such a short time and that unsettles a  social system here are people that were  basically rural people and farm people  all of a sudden in inner cities in  another atmosphere namely in the north  and  that was a disruption of those cities  partly mostly because of course the  white people didn't like it and then  there's all the white flight  any rate that's what then creates the  opportunities for people to to  do battle with this with these power  structures and in that context the  moderate conservatives are willing to  bend  and  in that sense we get reforms in our  system which may be lost later which  very definitely can be lost later and  that's the uh  the kind of the story of what's happened  with the reagan years that is the  disruption is gone  patriotism is back there's no wars in  that context  uh the conservatives then all of a  sudden talk about how well these were  just liberal experiences just  experiments these were just a bunch of  intellectuals trying out ideas in other  words they totally ignore the fact that  that these things were all done in the  face of real real pressure and they are  of course trying to undercut all those  programs let's turn now to the local  level what is the difference between the  power structure on the local and the  national level and the differences  between the possibilities for individual  participation and impact locally and  nationally i think that local power  structures are rooted not in large  corporations that produce a product  which is which is where the national  power structure is rooted but rather  local power structures are  rooted in real estate in land in  development at the local level we have  what we like to call a growth machine  the national level we have a power elite  rooted in large corporation at the local  level we have a growth machine that's  rooted in the attempt to increase the  value of land and what they want is  growth  and which intensifies the use of their  land  now  that means then that we have to look at  a complex of land owners  developers real estate sales people and  so on and mortgage banks savings and  loans tied around ground development  that's what happens at the local level  all apart the key issues to a local  power structure are the water board  the parking and transportation board the  planning board the zoning board that's  the real politics for  uh local power structure the other  issues are for them  uh symbolic some things that concern  other citizens but the local power  structure is concerned with with  growth now the local power structure  does relate to the national power  structure in a very important way and  that is that what a local power  structure tries to do is to attract  national capital that is they try to  prepare the land  their area so it will be attractive to  the new building the new high-rise  building that general motors wants to  put up somewhere so we try to as a local  city they try to say look we have the  cheapest land that you could want we  have a very nice and docile labor force  we have low taxes so all these local  growth machines compete with each other  to try to get the general motors factory  the exxon office building or more  generally the new campus of the  university of texas they try to get the  new  new defense department installation  because if you bring one of those major  capital kinds of structures into your  city it will mean new jobs for people it  will mean then more money  more spending for the local retailers  and you will then your land will be  valuable because now the local retailer  is making more money so you can charge  him more rent on the building that  you've rented it  so i think that's really important to  understand that that  uh the the land based nature of local  power structures  versus this more capital-based nature of  totally capital-based nature of the  national power structure i've just then  suggested that there's a lot of  commonality between them that they that  they have reasons to work together but  you also must understand that there's  tensions between them because if say the  labor force becomes very radical in a  given city  general motors might say okay we're  leaving  we're going overseas or we're going to  tennessee  and that at that point they can leave  behind a broken local growth machine so  the local growth machine often finds  itself angry with national level capital  when it leaves so there's tension  between them  as well  as well as cooperation  now  it's also important to understand that  at the local level  the primary tension  is created between the growth machine as  it attempts to expand  and the rest of the people  who are trying to live their lives in  their neighborhoods  at the national level we have a lot of  conflict  between the owners of corporations and  other people as workers as workers we  want  more wages which of course for the  capitalist point of view is inflationary  we want uh various kinds of benefits  which from their point of view might  make us lazy and not work as hard and so  on so we have a capital labor tension  in national politics that underlies a  lot of the fights over welfare and  inflation and so on but at the local  level the fight is between a growth  machine and the rest of us who as  neighbors as as living in a neighborhood  the growth machine's tendency is to move  out and move into neighborhoods either  to put in office buildings or to put in  apartment buildings that is say you're  living in this nice wonderful  neighborhood you've bought this house  all people have these you know single  family dwelling and in comes the growth  machine because there's more people now  and they can make more money if they put  a big apartment building in the middle  of your little area and you don't like  that  the constant site is between then the  growth machine and neighborhoods  and that really animates city politics  now  this this conflict can be very liberal  or it can be very reactionary  that is that sometimes neighborhoods are  protecting themselves from toxic waste  from tremendous overcrowding and a  liberal person can certainly understand  that and identify with that kind of with  the neighbors in that conflict but other  times  the conflict is such that it's very  reactionary because the neighbors  are trying to keep out minority people  or they are in essence saying uh we  don't want a more dense zoning because  we like having our two acre lot but by  having a two acre lot they're making  uh housing prices more expensive for  everybody in the city so  in other words you can't then find  enough land to build houses for for for  people of more average income  so  what i'm then saying is that you have to  look at this constant conflict between a  growth machine and neighborhoods  but at the same time you don't  necessarily say one side is going to be  or that the neighborhoods are going to  be progressive  now what then happens in local politics  is  that some neighborhood issue will come  up that is in some way the growth  machine will encroach upon a  neighborhood they're going to put a  bigger freeway in they're going to put a  high-rise in they're going to put a  factory that belches smoke into the  neighborhood and one of those they're  going to put a noisy installation in  the neighborhood will be incredibly  galvanized it will  fight  but once the fight is over in their  neighborhood they tend to lapse back  into their ordinary routines and of  course in terms of something i said  earlier you can now see my  generalization about disruption  people go into action when their  routines are disrupted and if i live in  this nice idyllic neighborhood now  there's going to be this smelly smoke  stacked plant or this noisy  office building or a big freeway right  near my house you've disrupted what i  see is my routine existence you see or  you've now overcrowded this school or  for some people you put minority kids in  this school by building this new  neighborhood you've disrupted what i saw  as my way of life and i become an  activist at the neighborhood level but  once my neighborhood is taken care of i  tend to lapse back  into quiet two once again so in a city  at different times we'll see different  neighborhoods  in action meanwhile the  structures and organizations  institutions of the local power  structure remain intact and remain in  basic control right and they are they're  usually of course rooted in the chamber  of commerce  but there's usually some watchdog tax  organization there's also some  real estate association there's also at  the national level incidentally an  organization called the urban land  institute that does the think tanking  for these local  growth machines  these local growth machines  have committees of the chamber of  commerce that parallel  the government committees that is for  instance for the planning commission  there's also a committee of the chamber  of commerce that looks at planning so  they have a whole parallel structure  to go alongside the government and  usually what happens then incidentally  is  that a person is legitimated as a city  leader by being a president of the  chamber by being the head of its  planning committee and then that person  then runs then excuse me that person  then gets appointed to the planning  commission  then after he or she has been on the  planning commission for a while which  then legitimates them as a city servant  they then run for city government  in one city in northern california  uh in a larger study that was done by  political scientists of 10 or 15 bay  area san francisco bay area city they  had the most wonderful process i mean  these people had such a lock on the  whole thing it was outrageous and that  the way it worked was you'd appoint a  person to the planning commission  midway and then of course you had your  people on the city council midway  through the person on the city council's  second term it was a two-term limit in  that city midway through the person's  second term they would resign and the  person from the planning commission  would be appointed by the rest of the  city council to the city council that  person would then two years later run as  an incumbent  for the next term and then of course do  his resignation after he won his second  election two years so so you had a  process then that everybody was running  was always an incumbent by means of of  appointment but you can see the process  from  a local growth machine member you get  involved in the chamber committee you  get appointed to the planning commission  then you run for city council that's  sort of the ideal career for the local  powershell  where you live have a progressive city  council how were people able to counter  this sort of machine or power structure  politics uh they seem to be able to  maintain it right they have been and but  the important thing to understand is  that where there have been successful  challenges to the growth machine there  has been very great environmental  concern  coupled with usually  student politics that is  when the university of california was  brought to santa cruz by the growth  machine as the perfect complement to the  tourism industry santa cruz is on the uh  wonderful beautiful beach  and its main  main thing for the growth machine is  tourism which means it doesn't want any  dirty industry spoiling but the  university was a clean industry  high level you know springing in  students you get that state budget was a  great coup for that local growth machine  to get uc santa cruz okay  and also furthermore the university runs  in the winter and tourism tends to run  into summer it's perfect but  the university was put inside the city  limits  for water hookups and other minor kind  of reasons  but the university is a giant trojan  horse inside the growth machine because  once this 18 year old vote was put in  the growth machine has not won a single  battle in santa cruz they wanted to  expand northward on the coast with a big  freeway and they would have taken in  huge ranch lands that would have become  40 50 000 people it's all going to be  parked they wanted to expand their  amusement area on a beautiful piece of  land it was stopped by the  environmentalists in the city plus the  student vote  so  i think the answer in santa barbara in  santa cruz in davis california with  progressive governments is a  student vote but right over the hill  from where i live is palo alto  california and in that particular city  the growth machine has also been stopped  in good measure but there you have to  understand that the people stop that  that was a suburban town  and the people who live in that town are  well-to-do people and it's their  neighborhood and they're not they fought  off the growth machine in that town by  getting experts to do a study which  showed that the developable land which  was on these these foothills  that that lane that was actually cheaper  for the city to buy that land and put it  in preserve than to pay for all of the  um  infrastructure that would be needed to  develop that land you see the standard  line to the growth machine is that it's  very good to have development because it  will create more jobs and bring in more  taxes when you do a cost benefit  analysis it often is the case but not  necessarily always that it costs more  to bring in this new growth to ordinary  citizens than they really do  get out of it  so in some places the growth machine has  been stopped there's also a wonderful  kind of  if i may use the word a dialectic that  goes on between the growth machine and a  neighborhood because growth machine will  create a new community  and they'll build all these houses and  the people will be very happy  everything's lovely but as you get more  and more people in the city at a certain  point the very city you've created as a  developer rises up and says you are  going to spoil what you originally  promised if you bring more people  and then all of a sudden there's a vote  against the developers  and they are stopped  so that kind of conflict happened  progressive governments now i want to  say one other thing related to this  growth machine business that that's  outside of a city level  it shows how how  how  different the disruptions can be  santa monica california also has a very  progressive government  what happened there was  it was a it was a city of little  single dwellings very nice little city  on the coast had some well to do but it  was a lot of it was middle class  a new freeway was developed in the late  60s i believe it was called the santa  monica freeway and what that freeway did  was make it possible to  for santa monica now to be a bedroom  community for lots more of la  which was a very attractive possibility  because of smog  that meant that the land in la in santa  monica was now tremendously more  valuable  if you put high rises on it so santa  monica was dramatically transformed into  this community of these huge  apartments  now once you have these people in these  apartments then of course you want to  charge a maximum rent and in the context  of the 70s their rents were going up  like crazy and some of these people were  retired people and they were middle  middle level people  what happened was that some aggressive  people moved to that city and they said  the sensible issue to organize this city  around is rent control  and they went out and ran a rent control  initiative now the first time they lost  but then  something called proposition 13 happened  in california that controlled property  taxes  part of winning that part of the growth  machines in california winning that  proposition 13 was to promise their  renters that that they wouldn't raise  their rents because now property taxes  would be lower but in fact rents went up  the following year in santa monica and  the progressive  politicians and  went back at another growth  a rent control initiative and this time  they won overwhelmingly plus their  candidates won for  for city council but the politics of of  santa monica have to be understood  in terms of of a very dramatically  rapidly changing  situation for the growth machine in  which it had  realized fantastic values thanks to this  freeway put up all these apartment  building but once they started to really  raise the rents on these people the  people rebelled through  rent control so rent control became the  device through which the growth the  growth machine was controlled  struggle etc and then we see the power  structure moving in again with the media  blitz and later campaigns putting a lot  of money right and getting some of these  progressives um out somehow it's a  constant sort of struggle right and in  the case of santa monica  where i think the progressive  politicians were very far-sighted very  clever about this and unders had it had  the kind of theory we're talking about  that allowed them to anticipate they  said where are these these growth  machine people going to strike back at  us they said they're going to strike  back on us talk about crime about more  police and so on so the next program  that the progressives developed was one  around a progressive way of dealing with  the question of crime and and uh  prevention of crime and so on and in  essence what they did was to take away  every issue that the  growth machine tried to use to beat them  at a certain point what happened in  santa monica that again shows this  flexibility  is that the developers would come to him  and say okay we want to develop certain  things what do you want and they'd say  well we'll let you build a building here  if you put a park over here if you put  child care over here and if you put some  low income housing over here we'll let  you develop it  the big developers would cost it out and  they'd say okay  the smaller developers would say it's  blackmail it's blackmail they were oh  they were angry but you know what today  i just read this in the last month or  two in  new york time 60 70 percent of of  american cities are now doing that very  kind of quote blackmail with developers  they're saying you're going to develop  okay you're going to pay for the school  you're going to pay for the new roads  you're going to put a park over here  you're going to put some low income  housing over here i wonder if austin's  doing this yet no no we aren't that  progressive maybe we have a lesson to  learn from now that's the way i mean the  developers will accommodate to that  see that here's the dynamic of america  the power because of our history and our  ideology the way the economic system  work  tremendous amount of power is  lodged then in this these private  economic institutions developers landed  interests at the local level  capital big corporations at the national  level the only thing that other people  can do is to gain some measure  of political power that forces these  economic powers to  in some way  accommodate  when you have in santa cruz you see  where where  progressive and liberal people have  political power what they use that for  is to then make trades  with these business people to to bring  amenities to the cities just recently  the two sides cut a deal in which the  the downtown business community and the  tourism industry agreed  to accept with with no fight an  amusement tax but that amusement tax is  going to be used to fund social services  in santa cruz that would probably be  lost if these reagan cutbacks  go through the ones that are currently  being debated  in congress and certainly were  eliminated reagan proposed getting rid  of all these support services so  we're able to be able to get these  social kinds of services and as i say  you can allow them to build certain  kinds of things if they'll do it in an  environmentally sound way and if they'll  also build other kinds of thing and so  you have this irony of where we're  getting sort of public amenities  but not through taxation which which our  ideology in america generally is such  that it's very hard then to to tax  anybody including these people and to go  after taxing and taxing them directly  they can often then use that against you  but if you say we'll let you develop  this if you give us the following  amenities  it works  well bill you've got a lecture coming up  here and a few minutes at the university  of texas we want to thank you for  my pleasure i've enjoyed it greatly it's  been wonderful and it seems to me that  the  basic to a lot of what you were saying  all the way through is that we do have  an american power structure but the  people have to struggle and organize and  demand and put pressure on it because  otherwise it's liable to do a job on  americans are all liberals in a classic  sense whether they think of themselves  as republicans or democrats and we have  to constantly build on that liberal  tradition use that liberal tradition and  use it against uh  uh the the power structure um it's it's  it's it's part of our culture there's a  history of activism at various levels of  our country there's america's basically  if i put it this way in closing america  is a conservative country with a liberal  ideology in which at various times  we get these chances and uh what you  have to do is make hay when those  chances come like in the 30s or or like  in the 60s  and i think it will come around again  and then the trick is to be there  basically demanding as much democracy as  much civil liberty  as you possibly can all of which are  perfectly legitimate things to ask for  in the united states of america  and that's alternative views for this  time  glad you could join us again  we'd like to thank our camera person  eric eubank our audio man kevin l west  and of course austin community  television ac tv
 

